  "observations": [
    "Opened actual untextured default isometric view: five-storey civic L upper wings, lower surrounding court, continuous gable/hip roof surfaces and regular framed windows. Lower hip roof retains visible segmented shading; reference ornament is not replicated.",
    "Opened actual material view. Frame, glass and paving remain distinct; light roof material makes inherited hip-corner faceting visible. Numeric five-storey design and open court differ from the ornate low-rise reference.",
    "Opened default7-storey bank material view: flat roof, tall side wing, separate lower hall, regular wood/window facade. No bank insignia or original rooms are invented; appearance differs from ornate reference.",
    "Opened actual medical material view: H roof silhouette and both large recesses visible; bridge joins real upper wings, seven repeated storeys, lower apron outside walls. Flat continuous roof has no crossing rectangular slabs.",
    "Opened default relay material view: open lower portico, two upper levels in L wings, real corner stair core and two low canopies. Original small benches remain at their own scale on the large apron; this is sparse authored use layout.",
    "Opened default terminal material view: six-storey L upper wings, open lower columns and broad apron at contracted scale. Small retained bench fixtures are visible; roof, frame, wall render and glazing remain separate.",
    "Opened full14-storey candidate material view: complete tall L silhouette is in frame, lower portico and canopies retain their separate heights, continuous long roof and repeated glazing remain visible. Studio ground-plane horizon is visible behind this much taller model.",
    "Opened two-storey dock material view. The lower level stays open apart from the declared stair core, upper L corridor and low canopies are visibly distinct, and original small benches retain their own scale.",
    "Opened actual medical ground cutaway. H wings and the middle connection are open inside; original bed fixtures and the separate stair core are visible. Interiors remain sparse author layouts, not original rooms.",
    "Opened3.8m stair component: real treads, continuous inclined undersides/wood rails and return landing. Long metal column ends are intended for floor-to-floor repetition and remain exposed in the standalone view.",
    "Opened4.4m stair component: additional risers, inclined rails and supported return landing retained. Standalone column ends extend to the next floor and are not cosmetic stair furniture.",
    "Opened actual4.2m stair closeup: the entire column ends, two flights and return platform are in frame; continuous underside and timber rails remain distinct.",
    "Reopened saved6m stair closeup to check the earlier provisional crop concern. All four long column ends and complete lower flight are visibly inside the saved PNG with margin; no supplementary framing or geometry edit is required.",
    "Opened6.6m stair closeup: complete tall column ends, two flights, continuous inclined undersides, rails and return platform are in frame. The actual asset envelope is7.86m including the retained floor-connecting ends.",
    "Opened full14-storey terminal back view: entire roof line, repeated framed windows and open lower portico remain in frame. Very small ground fixtures retain their original scale; this is the numeric-contract author candidate.",
    "Opened actual expanded15-storey bank form separately. The complete tall bar and lower annex are in frame; floor cadence and continuous flat roofs are preserved. Repeated sparse facade is an authored numeric-contract form, not the ornate reference building.",
    "Opened actual compact9-storey medical form separately. Both large H recesses and the continuous connecting roof are visible; the independent corner stair core remains beside the facade without filling the central court.",
    "Opened actual public expanded dock creation: lower open apron/portico, upper L wings, corner stair core and low canopies visible. Native benches and width cadence retain their own dimensions.",
    "Opened actual public quarter-turn placement: both complete dock instances visible with different90-degree headings and intact L plan/canopies; instance count doubles from94to188, matching the separately checked transforms.",
    "Opened actual purpose-palette dock view. Roof changes blue-gray, paving green-gray, wall render warm tan and glass blue separately; geometry, open lower level and fixtures remain. This is a base-color inspection, not a calibrated PBR comparison.",
    "Opened first real-capture contact sheet:2police and10bank forms, including compact/expanded12–15storeys and ordinary7/8storeys. Full silhouettes are visible; default PBR versus base-color views are explicitly labeled and not a color/scale comparison.",
    "Opened second real-capture contact sheet:4remaining ordinary bank forms, all6medical H forms, fixed interchange and compact relay. Both medical recesses remain visibly open in all6thumbnails; layer counts vary while family footprints remain legible.",
    "Opened third real-capture contact sheet:expanded relay,3terminal forms including fixed supplied dimensions, both14-storey interstellar and both2-storey dock forms. All8complete silhouettes are visible with lower open porticos and retained canopies; every screenshot is independently framed."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Default contact-sheet entries use material views, while nondefault entries and MCP geometry closeups use base-color inspection without studio lighting. Views have independent framing and are not a calibrated scale or color comparison. All32 actual material GLBs have independent material/alpha checks.",
    "Civic hip-roof corner faceting and regular repeated bays retain the authored parent appearance. Floors are sparse author layouts with a few original purpose fixtures; no original room plan is invented.",
    "The reference illustrates ornate low-rise buildings while the numerical catalogue contract specifies large footprints and2–15storeys. Numeric-contract candidates are not claimed as a visual match or human art acceptance.",
